I firmly believe that veterans' benefits should be expanded to include comprehensive mental health services for all veterans, regardless of their discharge status. It's a matter of basic human decency and a moral obligation to ensure that those who have served their country receive the care they need to heal and thrive.

The reality is that military service can have a profound impact on a person's mental health, regardless of their discharge status. The trauma, stress, and exposure to combat can lead to a range of mental health issues, from PTSD to depression, anxiety, and substance abuse. And yet, many veterans are left without access to the care they need because of their discharge status.

It's unacceptable that we would deny mental health services to those who have served their country, simply because they didn't receive a "honorable" discharge. This is not only a moral failing, but it's also a practical one. When veterans are left without access to care, they are more likely to struggle with their mental health, which can lead to increased rates of homelessness, unemployment, and even suicide.

Furthermore, expanding mental health services to all veterans, regardless of discharge status, is not only the right thing to do, it's also a sound investment in our national security. When veterans receive the care they need, they are more likely to be productive members of society, contributing to their communities and contributing to the economy. And when they are struggling with their mental health, they are more likely to be a burden on the system, requiring costly interventions and services.

